2.1 Before You Begin
====================

Install ‘git’, ‘make’, GNU Texinfo (which provides ‘makeinfo’), and
Emacs 31 or newer (https://www.gnu.org/software/emacs/download.html).
The recommended Omnivox speech server also needs the Rust toolchain,
Python 3, a C and C++ toolchain, CMake, and Clang with libclang.

   To check the Emacs found by your shell, run:

     emacs --version

   The first line should begin with ‘GNU Emacs 31’ or a higher version
number.  If the command is not found or reports an older version,
install a supported Emacs or locate its executable.  You can check a
specific executable in the same way:

     /path/to/emacs-31/bin/emacs --version

   On GNU/Linux, install ‘pkg-config’ and the ALSA development headers.
On macOS, install the Xcode command-line tools.  WSL2 has additional
Docker and Windows requirements described in *note Building Omnivox For
WSL2::.

   Choose a permanent parent directory and clone Emacsvox and Omnivox
beside one another:

     cd /path/where/you/keep/source
     git clone https://github.com/bartbunting/emacsvox
     git clone https://github.com/bartbunting/omnivox

   The Emacsvox checkout is the installation directory.  It builds in
place and does not create a second tree to copy elsewhere.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Building And Testing The Speech Server,  Next: Quick Installation,  Prev: Obtaining Emacsvox,  Up: Installation

2.2 Install And Check Omnivox
=============================

On GNU/Linux or macOS, build and install Omnivox from its checkout:

     cd /path/where/you/keep/source/omnivox
     make build
     make install
     omnivox --check

   ‘make install’ normally places the executable below ‘~/.cargo/bin’.
Make sure that directory is on ‘PATH’ before running the check.  No
proprietary speech runtime is needed for the recommended GNU/Linux or
macOS route.

   Under WSL2, build the staged Windows runtime from a clean Emacsvox
checkout:

     cd /path/where/you/keep/source/emacsvox
     make windows-omnivox
     ./servers/omnivox --check

   The check plays a tone followed by speech.  When you hear both, the
speech server is ready.  If either is missing, use *note Backend
Diagnostics And Responsibilities: Backend Diagnostics And Ownership.
before continuing.  *Note Speech Backends:: contains the complete
platform matrix, build details, and compatibility alternatives.  Native
Windows Emacs support remains a preview; consult that matrix before
choosing it for a first installation.


