3 Basic Usage
*************

Now that Emacsvox is speaking, this chapter helps you become comfortable
with the essential keys and feedback.  It begins with a short guided
session, then introduces the reading and speech controls you are most
likely to need.

* Menu:

* Overview::                     Understand the main kinds of Emacsvox feedback.
* Working::                      Understand feedback while editing and completing text.
* Reading::                      Reading chunks of information.
* Speech System::                Adjust speech for the way you are working.
* Voice-lock::                   Hear document and program structure through voice changes.
* Status Information::           Ask about the current buffer, position, and Emacs state.

3.1 Key Notation And Emacs Words
================================

Emacs documentation writes ‘C-’ for the Control key and ‘M-’ for the
Meta key, which is usually Alt or Option.  For example, ‘C-n’ means hold
Control and press ‘n’.  A sequence such as ‘C-e l’ means press ‘C-e’,
release it, and then press ‘l’.

   ‘M-x’ prompts for an Emacs command by name, and <RET> means the
Return or Enter key.  A _buffer_ is the Emacs workspace containing text
or an application.  _Point_ is the current editing position—the place
affected by movement and editing commands.

   If Emacs itself is new to you, ‘C-h t’ opens its interactive
tutorial.  You can return to this chapter whenever the tutorial refers
to buffers, point, windows, or key sequences.

3.2 Your First Few Minutes
==========================

Try these actions in the clean session started during installation:

  1. Press ‘C-n’ and ‘C-p’ to move down and up by one line.  Emacsvox
     speaks the line you reach.

  2. Press ‘C-e l’ to hear the current line without moving.

  3. Press ‘C-e s’ whenever you want to stop speech immediately.

  4. Press ‘C-h k’ followed by any key to hear what that key does.

  5. Press ‘C-h m’ to hear about the modes and commands available in the
     current buffer.

  6. Press ‘C-h C-e’ to open the Emacsvox overview.

   These commands are enough to begin exploring safely.  The rest of
this chapter adds reading controls and speech settings as you need them.
The generated *note (emacsvox-reference)Emacsvox Keymaps:: records the
complete default keymaps, while live help remains authoritative when a
package or personal configuration changes a binding.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Overview,  Next: Working,  Up: Basic Usage

3.3 Emacsvox Overview
=====================

Emacsvox adds spoken feedback to Emacs editing and to applications that
run inside Emacs.  Movement speaks the corresponding character, word,
line, or larger unit.  Commands announce useful results and state
changes, while application integrations present structures such as
messages, search results, tables, source code, and Web pages.

   Voice Lock uses changes in speech characteristics to make text
structure audible.  For example, comments, quoted strings, reserved
words, headings, and links can sound different without an explanation
being inserted into the text.  See *note Voice Lock:: for configuration
and *note Web Browsing:: for a task-oriented example in EWW.

   Pronunciation dictionaries let you adjust how a name, technical term,
or programming-language word is spoken.  A dictionary can apply
everywhere or only in a particular mode or file; see *note
(emacsvox-reference)emacsvox-pronounce::.

   Short auditory icons and tones mark events such as opening or
deleting a file, finishing an action, receiving mail, or displaying
completions.  They supplement speech and become especially useful as
familiar events no longer need a full spoken explanation.  See *note
(emacsvox-reference)emacsvox-sounds:: for the available sound support.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Working,  Next: Reading,  Prev: Overview,  Up: Basic Usage

3.4 Using Emacs Buffers
=======================

As you type, character echo can speak individual characters and word
echo can speak a word when you finish it.  Completion interfaces
announce candidates, the inserted completion, and the item currently
selected.  Prompts and error messages are spoken through the same Emacs
feedback that appears on screen.

   Most everyday feedback happens automatically.  Emacsvox also provides
three useful groups of explicit commands:

   • Reading commands speak a character, word, line, region, or larger
     portion of a buffer without requiring you to move first.  Their
     names usually begin with ‘emacsvox-speak-’, and their default keys
     use the main ‘C-e’ prefix.(1)

   • Speech-system commands change settings such as rate, punctuation,
     and echo.  Their names usually begin with ‘tts-’, and many are
     grouped under ‘C-e d’.(2)

   • Help commands describe the active modes, available keys, and
     Emacsvox integrations in the buffer where you are working.

   The following sections introduce a practical starter set.  Use live
Help or the generated reference when you need the complete commands for
a particular application.

   ---------- Footnotes ----------

   (1) The prefix is inherited from Emacspeak, where C-e was mnemonic
for the project name.

   (2) Historically, d was mnemonic for DECtalk; the prefix now contains
engine-independent speech controls.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Reading,  Next: Speech System,  Prev: Working,  Up: Basic Usage

3.5 Reading And Navigating
==========================

Ordinary Emacs movement speaks the text crossed by point: moving by a
character, word, line, or paragraph presents the corresponding unit.
The commands below let you inspect useful units without first moving.
They use the default ‘C-e’ prefix.

   This is deliberately a starter set, not a second complete key
reference.  If a binding below differs in your session, use ‘C-h k’ on
that key and check your customizations.  The complete generated defaults
are in the *note (emacsvox-reference)Emacsvox Keymaps::.

‘C-e c’

     ‘emacsvox-speak-char’ speaks the character at point.
‘C-e w’

     ‘emacsvox-speak-word’ speaks the current word.  Repeating it at the
     same position spells the word.
‘C-e l’

     ‘emacsvox-speak-line’ speaks the current line.
‘C-e [’

     ‘emacsvox-speak-paragraph’ speaks the current paragraph.
‘C-e ]’

     ‘emacsvox-speak-page’ speaks the current page.
‘C-e r’

     ‘emacsvox-speak-region’ speaks the active region between point and
     mark.
‘C-e i’

     ‘emacsvox-speak-rest-of-buffer’ speaks from point to the end of the
     buffer.
‘C-e b’

     ‘emacsvox-speak-buffer’ speaks the entire current buffer.
‘C-e s’

     ‘tts-stop’ stops current speech.
‘C-e C-d’

     ‘emacsvox-toggle-show-point’ toggles the aural indication of point
     within spoken text.

   Many speaking commands accept a prefix argument to limit speech to
the text before or after point.  Use ‘C-h k’ on a command before relying
on those variants, because their exact argument behavior is documented
with the command.


