3.6 Speech System Commands
==========================

Start with the controls in this section when speech is too fast, too
detailed, or inappropriate for the current task.  Most settings apply
only to the current buffer.  Press ‘C-u’ before a command when its help
says that a prefix argument changes the default for all buffers.

   The generated *note (emacsvox-reference)Emacsvox Keymaps:: contains
the complete speech keymap, including specialist server and
text-chunking controls.

* Menu:

* Controlling Echo::             Choose character, word, and line feedback while typing.
* Speech Output Control::        Adjust rate and how textual detail is presented.
* Miscellaneous::                Stop, silence, restart, or identify the speech server.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Controlling Echo,  Next: Speech Output Control,  Up: Speech System

3.6.1 Choose What Is Echoed While Typing
----------------------------------------

Character echo speaks each character you type.  Word echo speaks a word
when you finish it, and optional line echo speaks a completed line.
Toggle each kind independently to suit the buffer: character feedback
may help in a form where accuracy matters, while word echo is often more
fluent for prose.

   The commands below affect the current buffer.  Press ‘C-u’ first to
change the corresponding default and apply it to the current buffer.

‘C-e d k’

     ‘emacsvox-toggle-character-echo’

     Toggle character echo.
‘C-e d w’

     ‘emacsvox-toggle-word-echo’

     Toggle word echo.
‘C-e d l’

     ‘emacsvox-toggle-line-echo’

     Toggle line echo.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Speech Output Control,  Next: Miscellaneous,  Prev: Controlling Echo,  Up: Speech System

3.6.2 Adjust Rate, Punctuation, Capitals, And Indentation
---------------------------------------------------------

Use these controls to match speech to the current material.  A faster
rate may suit familiar prose; full punctuation can help when inspecting
code or exact text.  Capitalization and indentation each have an on/off
control and a separate choice of spoken or tonal presentation.

   The commands affect the current buffer unless their interactive help
describes a global prefix form.  Use ‘C-h k’ on a key before changing
the global default.

‘C-e d r’

     ‘tts-set-rate’

     Enter a speech rate for the current buffer.
‘C-e d f’

     ‘tts-set-character-scale’

     Set the rate multiplier used when speaking individual characters.
‘C-e d DIGIT’

     ‘tts-set-predefined-rate’

     Choose one of ten predefined rates with ‘0’ through ‘9’.
‘C-e d p’

     ‘tts-set-punctuations’

     Choose ‘some’, ‘all’, or ‘none’ punctuation.
‘C-e d s’

     ‘tts-toggle-split-caps’

     Toggle splitting of mixed-capital identifiers into separate words.
‘C-e d c’

     ‘tts-toggle-caps’

     Toggle capitalization indication.
‘C-e d C’

     ‘emacsvox-set-capitalization-presentation’

     Choose speech, a tone, both, a custom Aural rule, or no added
     indication.  The command previews representative text before you
     finish.

   Indentation feedback is especially useful in source code and other
structured text.  You can switch it off independently or choose how
indentation is presented.

‘C-e d i’

     ‘emacsvox-toggle-audio-indentation’

     Toggle indentation feedback.
‘C-e d I’

     ‘emacsvox-set-indentation-presentation’

     Choose a spoken column, one of the tone styles, speech and tone
     together, a custom Aural rule, or no added indication.  The command
     previews an indented sample before you finish.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Miscellaneous,  Prev: Speech Output Control,  Up: Speech System

3.6.3 Stop, Silence, Restart, Or Identify Speech
------------------------------------------------

Stopping speech ends the current output without disabling future
feedback.  Quiet mode is useful when you temporarily want to keep
working without speech.  Restart the server after correcting a backend
problem or changing the selected synthesizer.

‘C-e s’

     ‘tts-stop’

     Stop speech now.
‘C-e d q’

     ‘tts-toggle-quiet’

     Toggle speech in the current buffer.  Press ‘C-u’ first to toggle
     it globally.
‘C-e C-s’

     ‘tts-restart’

     Stop and restart the selected speech server.

   To hear which speech adapter is running, use:

‘C-e d V’

     ‘tts-speak-version’

     Speak the selected adapter and its version information.

   Use ‘C-h m’ or the generated keymap reference for server selection,
repeated-pattern cleanup, text splitting, language selection, and direct
engine controls.  Those specialist settings are intentionally outside
this starter guide.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Voice-lock,  Next: Status Information,  Prev: Speech System,  Up: Basic Usage

3.7 Voice Lock Mode
===================

Voice Lock is enabled automatically in supported buffers.  It maps faces
and other structural text properties to speech personalities, allowing
comments, strings, headings, links, and other elements to sound distinct
without adding words to the document.

   The exact personalities depend on the selected speech engine and your
Aural Presentation settings.  Try Voice Lock in source code, Org, Info,
or EWW to hear the active mode's structure.

   To disable Voice Lock globally, run ‘M-x global-voice-lock-mode’ or
add this form to your Emacs init file:

     (global-voice-lock-mode -1)

   The current binding and command reference are in *note
(emacsvox-reference)Emacsvox Keymaps::.  For detailed configuration, see
*note Voice Lock::.  For historical context on Aural CSS, see *note
(emacsvox-heritage)Audio Formatting And Aural CSS::.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Status Information,  Prev: Voice-lock,  Up: Basic Usage

3.8 Find Out Where You Are
==========================

Use these commands when you need context without moving point.  They
repeat the last message, identify the current buffer and modes, or
report your position.  Less common status commands are listed in the
*note (emacsvox-reference)Emacsvox Keymaps::.

‘C-e a’

     ‘emacsvox-speak-message-again’

     Repeat the last Emacs message.
‘C-e m’

     ‘emacsvox-speak-mode-line’

     Speak the mode line, including the buffer name and major mode.
‘C-e M’

     ‘emacsvox-speak-minor-mode-line’

     Speak the active minor-mode information.
‘C-e C-x’

     ‘emacsvox-speak-header-line’

     Speak the header line when the current buffer has one.

‘C-e C-w’

     ‘emacsvox-speak-window-information’

     Describe the current Emacs window arrangement.
‘C-e f’

     ‘emacsvox-speak-buffer-filename’

     Speak the current file name, or the default directory when the
     buffer is not visiting a file.
‘C-e C-l’

     ‘what-line’

     Report the current line number and position in the buffer.
‘C-e =’

     ‘emacsvox-speak-current-column’

     Speak the current column.
‘C-e %’

     ‘emacsvox-speak-current-percentage’

     Announce the percentage into the current buffer.


